                <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
                <h3>Q1: What is the cheapest time to fly from Austin to London? </h3>
                <p>January and February offer the lowest fares, typically $500-$850 round-trip in economy. For summer visits, book 45-70 days ahead and target April or September. Tuesday and Wednesday departures consistently undercut weekend prices by a meaningful margin. </p>

                <h3>Q2: Are there direct flights from Austin to London? </h3>
                <p>British Airways operates seasonal nonstop service from Austin to London Heathrow, typically from spring through autumn. Flight duration is approximately 9 to 9.5 hours. During winter months, all itineraries require connections through Dallas, Houston, Chicago, or other hubs. </p>

                <h3>Q3: Do I need a visa to visit London from the US? </h3>
                <p>United States citizens do not require a visa for short tourist stays in the UK. You may stay up to six months. A valid passport is required. The UK operates outside the Schengen Area, so separate entry requirements apply if combining with Continental Europe. </p>

                <h3>Q4: How much baggage can I bring on an Austin to London flight? </h3>
                <p>Most full-service carriers include one checked bag up to 50 pounds and one carry-on plus a personal item on transatlantic itineraries. British Airways includes baggage on most fares. US legacy carriers often charge $35 for the first checked bag. Always verify your specific airline's policy. </p>

                <h3>Q5: What is the best way to get from Heathrow Airport to central London? </h3>
                <p>The Elizabeth Line offers the best balance of cost and speed, reaching central London in 30-45 minutes for approximately £12-£15. The Heathrow Express takes 15 minutes to Paddington for £25. The Tube costs £6 but takes 50-60 minutes. Taxis run £50-£80. </p>

                <h3>Q6: Can I use US dollars in London, or do I need British pounds? </h3>
                <p>The United Kingdom uses the pound sterling exclusively, and US dollars are not accepted. ATMs are abundant throughout London, and credit cards work at most establishments. Contactless payment is widely preferred. Carry some cash for small purchases at markets or older pubs. </p>

                <h3>Q7: Is London safe for American tourists? </h3>
                <p>London is generally very safe for visitors, though petty theft occurs in tourist-heavy areas like Oxford Circus, Covent Garden, and on the Tube. Watch for phone snatching by cyclists. Violent crime against tourists is rare, and police presence is substantial in central areas. </p>

                <h3>Q8: What is the best area to stay in London for first-time visitors? </h3>
                <p>Covent Garden places you near West End theaters and major attractions. South Kensington offers museum access and elegant surroundings. Shoreditch suits younger travelers seeking nightlife and street art. Notting Hill provides a village atmosphere with excellent dining. Choose based on your priorities. </p>

                <h3>Q9: How early should I arrive at Austin airport for my London flight? </h3>
                <p>Arrive three hours before scheduled departure for international flights from Austin-Bergstrom. During peak summer travel or holiday weekends, consider arriving 3.5 hours early. TSA PreCheck and CLEAR members can significantly reduce security wait times. </p>

                <h3>Q10: What happens if my flight to London is delayed or cancelled? </h3>
                <p>Airlines must provide meals and accommodation for qualifying overnight delays depending on cause and duration. For cancellations, EU regulations protect passengers on EU-registered carriers, while most major airlines extend comparable protections voluntarily. Contact your airline immediately and maintain travel insurance for additional coverage. </p>





